  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 930 PM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... An upstream complex of showers and storms will \ncontinue moving south into the southern Appalachians overnight \ntonight, but the most intense widespread activity looks to stay west \nof central NC.\n\nThe line of storms with gusty winds associated with the first MCS \nhas largely moved south of our region and is now over northern SC \nand far SE NC. There is a cool, stable pool in its wake with \ntemperatures in the 70s and plenty of CIN across central NC. Only \nsome light to moderate stratiform rain trails the initial line, and \nthis will also push to our south over the next few hours. \n\nAnother MCS is located over the TN Valley and southern Appalachians, \nwith a leading bowing line of storms stretching across KY, eastern \nTN and far western NC. While earlier high-res guidance was mixed on \nthe track and strength of this storm complex and whether it would \nimpact portions of central NC later tonight, based on latest radar \ntrends and latest runs of the HRRR, as well as the widespread \nstability across the area, the worst of it appears to pass to our \nwest overnight. Still can't rule out a gusty shower and perhaps \nrumble of thunder over mainly the western Piedmont between about 02z \nand 05z.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... More excessive heat is possible on Wednesday, \nparticularly over the southern Coastal Plain, but confidence in \ndetails is still too low for a Heat Advisory at this time.\n\nWithin the complex forecast pattern, there are many failure modes \nfor Wed in terms of dangerous heat. The lingering cloud cover from \nupstream MCS activity over the Mid-Atlantic will be more probable \nover the Carolinas on Wed and could temper highs by a few degrees \nand prevent reaching the thermodynamic potential through the \nafternoon. This factor along with the locations of outflow \nboundaries from convection expected later today will likely keep \ntemp/dew point forecast confidence low until later tonight when the \ndusts settles. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... The heat and humidity will continue into late week, \nwith daily afternoon chances for isolated thunderstorms. \n\nHot and humid conditions will persist through late week, accompanied \nby daily chances for isolated afternoon thunderstorms. High \ntemperatures Thursday and Friday will range from the low 90s in the \nnorthwest to the mid/upper 90s elsewhere. High dew points in the mid \n70s will yield peak heat indices in the low to mid 100s.\n\nRelief arrives over the weekend as a frontal boundary approaches \nThursday and pushes through late Friday. Expect isolated to \nscattered showers and storms in the afternoon and evening. Highs \nwill be in the upper 80s to low 90s, keeping heat indices \npredominantly below triple digits. However, southwest flow quickly \nreturns early next week, bringing another round of temperatures in \nthe mid/upper 90s paired with triple-digit heat indices for much of \nthe area.",
